<h1>Git Status Frame</h1>
<p>A suite is code, and code lives in a repository. When several people work on the same suite, or when an
	<a class="text-accent" href="/ai-agents/">AI agent</a> pushes new tests overnight, the copy a controller has
	loaded can fall behind what the repository holds, and a run then exercises old tests without anyone noticing.
	The <strong>Git Status</strong> panel, in the right pane below the configuration, answers that question for
	every loaded suite: it compares the suite's checkout with the <code>main</code> branch of its remote and shows
	the result as one line per repository.</p>
<p>The panel appears when a loaded suite sits inside a git repository. The tutorial suite cloned in
	<a class="text-accent" href="/setup/getting-started/">Getting started</a> does, so with the suite loaded the
	panel shows its row:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/git-status-frame/git-status-up-to-date.png", "Git Status panel with one row: the orcraft-test-suite-tutorial repository, a green Up to date with remote origin/main chip, and the subject of the latest commit", "large") }}
<p>The row reads from left to right: a coloured bar and the repository name, a refresh icon, the status chip
	<strong>Up to date with remote origin/main.</strong> in green, and the subject of the commit both sides are on.
	Green means the checkout is at the remote's <code>main</code>; a run now exercises the suite as the repository
	holds it. The status is read when the suite is loaded and again when the refresh icon is clicked; it contacts
	the remote to learn where <code>main</code> is, so it needs network access to the repository's host.</p>

<h2 id="behind-the-remote">A checkout behind the remote</h2>
<p>When someone has pushed to <code>main</code> since the suite was cloned, the row changes:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/git-status-frame/git-status-behind.png", "Git Status panel with the orcraft-test-suite-tutorial row in yellow, a Behind remote origin/main chip, a pull icon, and the remote and local commit hashes side by side", "large") }}
<p>The bar and the chip turn yellow, the chip reads <strong>Behind remote origin/main.</strong>, and a pull icon
	appears after the refresh icon. Instead of a commit subject the row now shows both sides:
	<strong>Remote Hash</strong> is the commit <code>main</code> is at and <strong>Local Hash</strong> the one the
	checkout is at. When the repository tags its releases, the tags take the place of the hashes.</p>
<p>The opposite case, a checkout with commits that are not on <code>main</code> yet, reads
	<strong>Ahead of remote origin/main.</strong> in the same yellow: the suite on this machine is not what the
	repository publishes, which is worth knowing before a result is shared. A suite whose folder is not a git
	checkout, or whose remote cannot be reached, shows <strong>Unable to access git repo.</strong> in grey.</p>

<h2 id="pulling-from-the-controller">Pulling from the controller</h2>
<p>Click the pull icon. The panel asks before touching the checkout:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/git-status-frame/confirm-pull-dialog.png", "Confirm Pull dialog asking whether to pull the latest changes from origin/main and fetch tags for orcraft-test-suite-tutorial, with Yes and No buttons", "small") }}
<p>Confirm with <strong>Yes</strong>. The controller pulls <code>main</code> and fetches the tags, and the row
	returns to green:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/git-status-frame/git-status-after-pull.png", "Git Status panel after the pull, with the row green again, the Up to date with remote origin/main chip and the latest commit subject", "large") }}
<p>The pull updates the files on disk; the tests already loaded in the tree are the ones read at load time. Click
	<strong class="text-danger">Reload</strong> in the tree's action bar to load the suite again from the updated
	files.</p>

<h2 id="the-collapsed-panel">The collapsed panel</h2>
<p>Click the arrow before the <strong>Git Status</strong> title to fold the panel into one line:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/git-status-frame/git-status-collapsed.png", "Collapsed Git Status panel showing only its title and the green Up to date with remote origin/main chip", "large") }}
<p>The chip keeps the state visible while the rest of the right pane takes the room. With several suites loaded
	from different repositories the panel has one row per repository, and the collapsed chip summarizes them.</p>

<h2 id="the-repository-in-the-results">The repository in the results</h2>
<p>The same information is recorded with every run. The results tree of the
	<a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/details-dialog/">Details dialog</a> carries <strong>Repo</strong>,
	<strong>Branch</strong>, <strong>Commit</strong> and <strong>File Path In Repo</strong> columns for each run,
	recorded from the suite's checkout. A result can therefore always be traced to the
	exact commit of the test that produced it, which is what makes a failure reproducible after the suite has moved
	on.</p>

<p>The next page, <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/details-dialog/">Details Dialog</a>, opens that dialog
	at every level of the tree and walks through what it holds.</p>
